# Engineering workflow

## Quality gates (run before every commit)

```
pnpm typecheck   # tsc --noEmit (TypeScript 6.0, strict)
pnpm lint        # eslint (flat config, typescript-eslint, jsx-a11y)
pnpm test        # vitest run
pnpm build       # next build — run at least once per milestone
```

Fix errors and warnings; do not suppress them without a comment explaining
why. `// eslint-disable` and `as any` require justification inline.

## TypeScript

- `strict: true`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes: true`.
- No `any` unless justified. Prefer `unknown` + Zod parsing at boundaries.
- Infer types from Drizzle schema and Zod schemas; do not hand-duplicate.
- TypeScript 6.0.x is pinned. Do not upgrade to 7 until `next build` and
  `typescript-eslint` support it without experimental flags (see ADR 0001).

## Testing

- Vitest 5 + Testing Library: domain logic, Zod schemas, tool definitions,
  context assembly, memory ranking, encryption helpers, model router, client
  components, synchronous server components.
- Playwright: auth flows, chat streaming, memory review, ventures/projects
  CRUD, and each environment mode (`compact`, `portable`, `command`) via
  device emulation. Run against `next build && next start`.
- Async server components are tested through Playwright, not Vitest.
- Test files sit next to the code as `*.test.ts(x)`; E2E in `e2e/`.

## Commits and branches

- One logical change per commit; imperative subject lines.
- Branches: `cursor/<descriptive-name>-<id>` for agent work.
- Keep the build green at every commit.

## Dependencies

- Do not add a package without a reason. Prefer platform APIs and existing
  dependencies. If a new package changes architecture, write an ADR.
- Pin majors in `package.json`; commit `pnpm-lock.yaml`.

## Documentation

- Architectural decisions → `docs/adr/NNNN-title.md` (Context, Decision,
  Consequences, Alternatives).
- Keep `docs/PHASE-1.md` checkboxes current as work lands.
- Do not write narrative comments that restate code. Comment intent,
  constraints, and trade-offs only.

## Validation

After a milestone, run the real application (dev or built) and exercise the
feature, including on a narrow viewport. Do not assume code works because it
compiles.
